            <ArticleTitle>[Sub capsular splenic hematoma in a sickle cell trait carrier. Case report].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1192-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">/S0034-98872011000900012</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Drepanocytic anemia is an uncommon hereditary disease in Chile. The heterozygous state of drepanocytic anemia or &quot;sickle trait&quot; has a frequency of 8% among Afro-Americans. A small number of patients carrying hemoglobin S are homozygous, with clinical manifestations of hemolytic anemia and thrombotic disease. Sickle trait is usually asymptomatic. We report a 59-year-old male who presented an acute abdominal pain and dyspnea while staying at high altitude. Six days later, an angio CAT scan showed the presence of a subcapsular splenic hematoma that was managed conservatively. Sickle cell induction with sodium metabisulphite was positive. Hemoglobin electrophoresis confirmed the sickle trait.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
